.TH PAM-AUTH-UPDATE 8 "2026-11-21" "pam-utils" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
pam-auth-update \- write the common PAM configuration from profiles
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pam-auth-update
.RB [ \-\-enable
.IR profile " ...]"
.RB [ \-\-disable
.IR profile " ...]"
.RB [ \-\-remove
.IR profile " ...]"
.RB [ \-\-package ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.RB [ \-\-root
.IR directory ]
.br
.B pam-auth-update \-\-list
.RB [ \-\-root
.IR directory ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pam-auth-update
writes the files
.IR /etc/pam.d/common-auth ,
.IR common-account ,
.IR common-password ,
.I common-session
and
.IR common-session-noninteractive .
The PAM configuration of a service includes these files and has little
else, so that what they say holds for all services.
.PP
What goes into the files comes from profiles, small files in
.I /usr/share/pam-configs
that packages with a PAM module bring along.
The program puts the lines of all enabled profiles together, in the order
of their priorities, and works out the jumps between them.
Nobody has to write a PAM stack by hand for that.
.PP
Called without options it writes the files again for the profiles that
are enabled.
A profile that was not there the last time is enabled if it says
.BR "Default: yes" .
Nothing is asked.
.PP
This is a rewrite in shell of the program of the same name in Debian.
The profiles and templates have the same format and the same files are
written for them, see
.B DIFFERENCES
below.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-enable " profile ..."
Enable the profiles.
It is an error to name one that is not installed.
.TP
.BI \-\-disable " profile ..."
Disable the profiles.
They stay disabled until they are enabled with
.BR \-\-enable .
.TP
.BI \-\-remove " profile ..."
Leave the profiles out as if they were not installed.
For the script of a package that is about to be removed, while its
profile is still there.
.TP
.B \-\-package
Says that the script of a package is calling.
If files were changed by hand, they are left alone and the exit status is
0 all the same.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Overwrite files that were changed by hand or that were not written by
this program.
The old file is kept with
.I .pam-old
at the end of its name.
.TP
.B \-\-list
Print the profiles in the order of their priorities, with their state and
description, and change nothing.
.TP
.BI \-\-root " directory"
Work on the system below
.IR directory .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a help message.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH CHANGES BY HAND
Each of the files has four marker lines.
The lines between the first and the second, and between the third and the
last, are the managed block: they are written by this program.
Everything else in the file can be changed, and modules can be added
before the first or after the last marker.
Such changes are kept.
.PP
A file whose managed block was changed, or that was not written by this
program, is not touched.
The program says which files these are and exits with status 2, and none
of the five files is written.
.B \-\-force
overwrites the managed block of such a file.
.SH PROFILES
A profile is a file with fields of the form
.BR "Key: value" .
A field can go on over several lines, the lines after the first are
indented.
Lines that start with # are ignored.
The name of the file is the name of the profile.
.TP
.B Name
A description, one line.
.TP
.B Default
.B yes
if the profile is enabled when it is seen for the first time.
.TP
.B Priority
A number.
Profiles with higher numbers come first in the stack.
.TP
.B Conflicts
Names of profiles this one cannot be enabled with, separated by commas.
Of two such profiles the one with the higher priority stays.
.TP
.BR Auth-Type ", " Account-Type ", " Password-Type ", " Session-Type
.B Primary
or
.BR Additional .
The lines of primary profiles decide whether the stack succeeds, they come
in front of the line that denies.
The lines of additional profiles come after it and are run whenever the
stack got that far.
.TP
.BR Auth ", " Account ", " Password ", " Session
The lines for the stack, without the type in front: a control and a
module, with its options.
In a control in square brackets
.B end
can be given as the number of lines to jump over, it stands for the end of
the block.
.TP
.BR Auth-Initial ", " Account-Initial ", " Password-Initial ", " Session-Initial
Lines that are used in place of the ones above if the profile is the first
one of its block.
.TP
.B Session-Interactive-Only
.B yes
if the lines for the session are not meant for
.IR common-session-noninteractive .
.PP
A profile with something else in it, such as a line without a module, is
an error and nothing is written.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The files were written, or
.B \-\-package
was given and files that were changed by hand were left alone.
.TP
.B 1
An error. Nothing was changed.
.TP
.B 2
Files were changed by hand and
.B \-\-force
was not given.
Nothing was changed.
.SH DIFFERENCES
What is not as in the program of Debian:
.IP \(bu 2
There is no dialog.
.IP \(bu 2
Debian's program takes options over that were added to or removed from a
line of the managed block.
Here a changed block stops the program.
.IP \(bu 2
The five files are all generated and checked before the first of them is
replaced.
.IP \(bu 2
A set of profiles without a primary module for
.B auth
or for
.B account
is refused.
With such a set every user would be let in.
.IP \(bu 2
.B end
is only replaced in the control of a line, not in the name of a module.
.IP \(bu 2
Of two conflicting profiles the one with the lower priority is dropped,
whichever of the two names the conflict.
.IP \(bu 2
.I /var/lib/pam
has the list of enabled profiles and a copy of each file as it was
written, not the files of Debian's program.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/pam.d/common-*
The files that are written.
.TP
.I /usr/share/pam-configs
The profiles.
.TP
.I /usr/share/pam/common-*
The templates new files are made from.
.TP
.I /var/lib/pam/enabled
The names of the enabled profiles.
.TP
.I /var/lib/pam/seen
The names of all profiles that were there at the last run.
.TP
.I /var/lib/pam/common-*
The files as they were written, to tell changes by hand.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pam.conf (5),
.BR pam (7)
.SH AUTHORS
